{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for exact clean pushed head 2dd9f72da31912fc501ee4bb7b108d6048bee58c and all seven named artifacts. Exact local and origin heads match, the branch is clean, base c679fb73 is an ancestor, git diff --check is clean, the three focused citation/configuration tests pass with -count=1, and go vet ./internal/app passes.\n\nARCHITECTURE: the affected boundary is layer 7, where internal/app runs repository-local Git and presents application state; the kernel and layer-5 durable meaning are unchanged. The architecture update correctly changes the layer-7 contract toward the durable property: a retirement is refused whenever the citation lookup cannot positively answer. The grep exit-1 versus other-failure distinction implements that property and is a sound contract change. Architecture therefore passes for the primary repair, but the claimed bounded repository-local configuration contract is not yet preserved by the exact head.\n\nSECURITY: the primary citation guard now fails closed correctly, and command-scope GIT_CONFIG_COUNT injection no longer silences it. One unresolved security defect remains. inheritedGitVariableAdmitted unconditionally preserves GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM, and GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM. I pointed GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL at a valid configuration containing core.fsmonitor = touch /tmp/gitseq-fsmonitor-review-2dd9f72d/executed, then ran the exact constructor-shaped status command: git --no-optional-locks -C repo-link status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=normal. It exited 0, executed the configured command, created the marker, and Git-appended arguments created two untracked files in the candidate checkout, which also corrupted the status result. The exact test-created files were removed with unlink, the temp directory with rmdir, and the checkout is clean. Thus ambient configuration still crosses the process-execution and status-integrity boundary. The request expressly requires a deliberately bounded Git environment/configuration contract; passing arbitrary scope files is not bounded merely because the variable names are allowlisted. Repair this without weakening the primary fail-closed rule or the legitimate safe-directory/config-isolation requirement, add a mutation-sensitive regression at the real status boundary, publish a new exact head and a fresh complete seven-artifact set, then request review again. Do not merge.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: keep the single repositoryLocalGit constructor, but simplify its policy around an explicit minimal trusted configuration contract rather than a split that forwards whole caller-selected configuration files. The constructor should admit only what the application deliberately needs and neutralize execution-affecting repository/config inputs at the command boundary; this reduces policy surface while preserving the required fail-closed outcome. No other simplification is worth expanding this repair.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:854ac714c0007541f7eedb8af175d304303fe35a","head":"2dd9f72da31912fc501ee4bb7b108d6048bee58c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8b4f952c9e6bb1547de1c623b6f3b82c90251860\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:80c37f8a0711d1a3aa1da91eefdd351972525b13\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:80c37f8a0711d1a3aa1da91eefdd351972525b13","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}